QUESTIONS.push(

  // ================= Software Engineering Principles & SDLC (50) =================
  { id: "sep-01", topic: "se-principles", q: "Software engineering is best defined as:", options: ["Writing code as quickly as possible with no process", "The systematic application of engineering principles to the design, development, testing, and maintenance of software", "Only the testing phase of a project", "A synonym for computer programming with no additional meaning"], answer: 1, exp: "Software engineering treats software development as a disciplined engineering activity, not just ad hoc coding." },
  { id: "sep-02", topic: "se-principles", q: "The Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) typically includes phases in the order:", options: ["Testing, coding, design, requirements", "Requirements gathering, design, implementation (coding), testing, deployment, and maintenance", "Deployment, requirements, testing, design", "Maintenance, requirements, testing, coding"], answer: 1, exp: "This is the conventional top-level sequence, though specific SDLC models vary in how strictly linear or iterative it is." },
  { id: "sep-03", topic: "se-principles", q: "The Waterfall model of SDLC is characterized by:", options: ["Constant iteration with no defined phases", "A sequential, linear progression through phases, where each phase must complete before the next begins, with little to no going back", "Building only a prototype with no final product", "Skipping requirements gathering entirely"], answer: 1, exp: "Waterfall's rigid, one-way flow is its defining (and most criticized) characteristic." },
  { id: "sep-04", topic: "se-principles", q: "A major drawback of the Waterfall model is:", options: ["It is too flexible to changing requirements", "Its inflexibility to changing requirements, since problems discovered late (e.g., in testing) can be costly to fix given the rigid sequential flow", "It cannot be used for any real project", "It requires no documentation at all"], answer: 1, exp: "Late-discovered issues in Waterfall often require expensive rework since earlier phases are treated as finished." },
  { id: "sep-05", topic: "se-principles", q: "The Iterative model of SDLC differs from Waterfall by:", options: ["Never revisiting any phase", "Developing the software through repeated cycles (iterations), refining and expanding it incrementally rather than in one single pass", "Skipping testing entirely", "Requiring the entire system to be designed before any coding begins, just like Waterfall"], answer: 1, exp: "Each iteration typically produces a more refined/complete version of the software." },
  { id: "sep-06", topic: "se-principles", q: "The Spiral model of SDLC particularly emphasizes:", options: ["Ignoring project risk entirely", "Risk analysis and management at each iteration/cycle, combining iterative development with systematic risk assessment", "A single, one-time, upfront risk assessment only", "Eliminating the need for prototyping"], answer: 1, exp: "The Spiral model's repeated risk-analysis loops make it well suited to large, high-risk projects." },
  { id: "sep-07", topic: "se-principles", q: "The V-Model (Verification and Validation model) extends the Waterfall model by:", options: ["Removing all testing phases", "Pairing each development phase with a corresponding testing phase, emphasizing early test planning alongside design", "Allowing testing to be skipped if the schedule is tight", "Requiring no requirements phase"], answer: 1, exp: "Each development stage on the V-Model's left side has a mirrored verification stage on the right side." },
  { id: "sep-08", topic: "se-principles", q: "The Prototyping model in software development is useful when:", options: ["Requirements are perfectly clear and fixed from day one", "Requirements are unclear or evolving, since a working prototype helps clarify and validate requirements with stakeholders early", "No stakeholder feedback is ever needed", "The project has zero budget for early exploration"], answer: 1, exp: "A prototype gives stakeholders something concrete to react to, surfacing misunderstandings before full-scale development." },
  { id: "sep-09", topic: "se-principles", q: "RAD (Rapid Application Development) as an SDLC model emphasizes:", options: ["Slow, methodical development with no reuse", "Fast development through reusable components and rapid prototyping, typically suited to smaller/well-understood projects with tight timelines", "Avoiding any prototyping whatsoever", "Only very large, multi-year projects"], answer: 1, exp: "RAD trades some rigor for speed, leaning heavily on component reuse and quick iteration." },
  { id: "sep-10", topic: "se-principles", q: "The Incremental model of SDLC delivers software by:", options: ["Delivering the entire system in one single release only", "Building and delivering the system in successive increments, each adding functionality to a working product", "Never delivering any working software until the very end", "Requiring all requirements to change with every increment"], answer: 1, exp: "Each increment adds working functionality, letting stakeholders see and use progress along the way." },
  { id: "sep-11", topic: "se-principles", q: "Functional requirements in a Software Requirements Specification (SRS) describe:", options: ["Only the system's performance targets", "What the system should DO — its specific behaviors, features and functions", "Only the system's color scheme", "Only the hardware the system will run on"], answer: 1, exp: "Functional requirements capture the concrete features/behaviors the system must provide." },
  { id: "sep-12", topic: "se-principles", q: "Non-functional requirements in an SRS describe:", options: ["Specific features/functions of the system", "Quality attributes of the system, such as performance, security, usability, and reliability, rather than specific features", "Only the project's budget", "Only the developers assigned to the project"], answer: 1, exp: "Non-functional requirements capture HOW WELL the system must perform, not WHAT it does." },
  { id: "sep-13", topic: "se-principles", q: "A feasibility study conducted early in a software project assesses:", options: ["Only the final testing results", "Whether the proposed project is technically, economically, and operationally viable before committing significant resources", "Only the visual design of the UI", "Only which programming language to use"], answer: 1, exp: "A feasibility study helps avoid investing heavily in a project that ultimately can't succeed." },
  { id: "sep-14", topic: "se-principles", q: "An SRS (Software Requirements Specification) document serves primarily to:", options: ["Provide the final source code", "Formally capture and communicate what the system must do, acting as a reference/agreement between stakeholders and developers", "Replace the need for any testing", "Only be used after the project is complete"], answer: 1, exp: "The SRS is a foundational reference document that guides design, implementation, and testing." },
  { id: "sep-15", topic: "se-principles", q: "Modularity in software design refers to:", options: ["Writing all code in a single undivided file", "Decomposing a system into separate, manageable, and ideally independent modules/components", "Avoiding any structure whatsoever", "Only applying to hardware, never software"], answer: 1, exp: "Modularity makes large systems easier to understand, develop, and maintain by breaking them into smaller pieces." },
  { id: "sep-16", topic: "se-principles", q: "Cohesion in software design refers to:", options: ["How dependent two different modules are on each other", "The degree to which the elements within a single module are functionally related and focused on a single well-defined purpose", "The total number of modules in a system", "The programming language used"], answer: 1, exp: "High cohesion means a module does one well-defined thing, rather than many unrelated things." },
  { id: "sep-17", topic: "se-principles", q: "Coupling in software design refers to:", options: ["How focused a single module's internal elements are", "The degree of interdependence between different modules; lower coupling generally makes modules easier to modify/reuse independently", "The total lines of code in a project", "The number of developers on a team"], answer: 1, exp: "Coupling describes inter-module dependency, complementing cohesion's intra-module focus." },
  { id: "sep-18", topic: "se-principles", q: "Good software design generally aims for:", options: ["Low cohesion and high coupling", "High cohesion (within modules) and low coupling (between modules)", "High cohesion and high coupling equally", "Neither cohesion nor coupling matters"], answer: 1, exp: "This combination produces modules that are focused internally and loosely connected externally — easier to maintain and reuse." },
  { id: "sep-19", topic: "se-principles", q: "Abstraction in software design means:", options: ["Exposing every internal implementation detail to callers", "Exposing only the essential details of a component while hiding its internal implementation complexity", "Removing all functionality from a component", "Writing code with no comments"], answer: 1, exp: "Abstraction lets users of a component interact with a simplified interface, without needing to understand its internals." },
  { id: "sep-20", topic: "se-principles", q: "Encapsulation in software design means:", options: ["Making all data globally accessible with no restriction", "Bundling data and the operations that act on it together, restricting direct external access to the internal state", "Separating data from all operations that use it", "Only applies to procedural programming, never object-oriented"], answer: 1, exp: "Encapsulation protects an object's internal state, exposing controlled access through defined operations/interfaces." },
  { id: "sep-21", topic: "se-principles", q: "Corrective software maintenance addresses:", options: ["Adding entirely new, unrelated features", "Fixing defects/bugs discovered after the software has been deployed", "Adapting the software to a new operating system", "Improving the software's internal code quality with no behavior change"], answer: 1, exp: "Corrective maintenance is reactive — responding to reported/discovered defects." },
  { id: "sep-22", topic: "se-principles", q: "Adaptive software maintenance involves:", options: ["Fixing bugs found by users", "Modifying software to keep working correctly as its environment changes (e.g., new OS version, changed regulations)", "Rewriting the entire system from scratch every year", "Deleting old, unused code with no other change"], answer: 1, exp: "Adaptive maintenance responds to external environmental changes, not internal defects." },
  { id: "sep-23", topic: "se-principles", q: "Perfective software maintenance involves:", options: ["Only fixing critical security bugs", "Enhancing the software with new features or improving performance/usability, beyond just fixing bugs", "Reverting the software to an earlier, worse version", "Ignoring user feedback entirely"], answer: 1, exp: "Perfective maintenance is about improvement, not just correction." },
  { id: "sep-24", topic: "se-principles", q: "Preventive software maintenance involves:", options: ["Waiting for problems to occur before taking any action", "Proactively modifying software (e.g., refactoring) to prevent future problems and improve maintainability, before issues actually occur", "Only occurring after a major system failure", "Removing all documentation"], answer: 1, exp: "Preventive maintenance is forward-looking, aiming to reduce future defect risk and maintenance cost." },
  { id: "sep-25", topic: "se-principles", q: "A software 'requirement' should ideally be:", options: ["Vague and open to broad interpretation", "Clear, unambiguous, verifiable, and traceable, so both stakeholders and developers share the same understanding", "Written only in highly technical jargon inaccessible to stakeholders", "Impossible to test or verify"], answer: 1, exp: "Well-written requirements reduce miscommunication and make it possible to verify the system meets them." },
  { id: "sep-26", topic: "se-principles", q: "Requirements elicitation is the process of:", options: ["Deleting unnecessary requirements after coding is finished", "Gathering requirements from stakeholders through techniques like interviews, surveys, and workshops", "Only reading existing source code to infer requirements", "Skipping stakeholder involvement entirely"], answer: 1, exp: "Elicitation is the upfront activity of discovering what stakeholders actually need." },
  { id: "sep-27", topic: "se-principles", q: "Requirements validation, as distinct from requirements elicitation, focuses on:", options: ["Gathering new requirements from stakeholders for the first time", "Confirming that the documented requirements accurately and completely reflect what stakeholders actually need", "Writing the source code that implements the requirements", "Testing the deployed production system"], answer: 1, exp: "Validation checks the already-documented requirements for correctness and completeness, after elicitation." },
  { id: "sep-28", topic: "se-principles", q: "A key benefit of choosing an iterative/incremental SDLC model over pure Waterfall for a project with evolving requirements is:", options: ["It locks requirements in permanently after the first phase", "The ability to incorporate feedback and adapt to changing requirements across cycles, rather than being locked into an initial fixed plan", "It eliminates the need for any testing", "It guarantees the project will never change scope"], answer: 1, exp: "Iterative/incremental models are specifically designed to accommodate evolving understanding and requirements." },
  { id: "sep-29", topic: "se-principles", q: "'Big design up front' is most closely associated with which SDLC approach?", options: ["The Agile/Scrum approach", "The Waterfall model, where design is largely finalized before implementation begins", "The Kanban method", "Extreme Programming (XP)"], answer: 1, exp: "Waterfall's sequential structure requires design to be substantially complete before moving to implementation." },
  { id: "sep-30", topic: "se-principles", q: "A software architecture describes:", options: ["Only the visual UI design", "The high-level structure of a system: its major components, their responsibilities, and how they interact", "Only the database schema", "Only the project's budget breakdown"], answer: 1, exp: "Architecture is the structural blueprint guiding how a system's parts fit together." },
  { id: "sep-31", topic: "se-principles", q: "Layered (n-tier) software architecture organizes a system into:", options: ["A single undivided block with no separation", "Hierarchical layers (e.g., presentation, business logic, data), where each layer typically only interacts with adjacent layers", "Only two layers: frontend and nothing else", "Layers with no defined interaction rules at all"], answer: 1, exp: "Layered architecture separates concerns into distinct, typically stacked responsibilities." },
  { id: "sep-32", topic: "se-principles", q: "Client-server architecture divides a system into:", options: ["Only a single unified component", "A client that requests services/resources, and a server that provides them, typically communicating over a network", "Two identical, interchangeable components with no distinct roles", "A system with no network communication at all"], answer: 1, exp: "This is one of the most fundamental architectural patterns, underlying most networked applications." },
  { id: "sep-33", topic: "se-principles", q: "The 'cost of change' in software engineering generally:", options: ["Decreases the later in the SDLC a defect or requirement change is discovered/made", "Increases the later in the SDLC a defect or requirement change is discovered/made, since more downstream work may need to be redone", "Is completely unrelated to when a change is made", "Is always zero regardless of timing"], answer: 1, exp: "This is a well-known principle motivating early testing and requirements validation." },
  { id: "sep-34", topic: "se-principles", q: "A software process model's main purpose is to:", options: ["Provide a random, undocumented approach to development", "Provide a structured, repeatable framework guiding how a software project moves from concept to delivery and maintenance", "Eliminate the need for any planning", "Only apply to very small, one-person projects"], answer: 1, exp: "Process models give teams a consistent, repeatable way to organize project work." },
  { id: "sep-35", topic: "se-principles", q: "Verification, as distinct from validation, in software engineering asks:", options: ["'Are we building the right product?'", "'Are we building the product right?' (conforming to specifications), whereas validation asks 'Are we building the right product?' (meeting actual user needs)", "Both questions mean exactly the same thing", "Neither question is relevant to software engineering"], answer: 1, exp: "Verification checks conformance to spec; validation checks fitness for actual user need." },
  { id: "sep-36", topic: "se-principles", q: "A design pattern in software engineering is:", options: ["A finished, ready-to-copy piece of code for one specific project", "A general, reusable solution template to a commonly recurring design problem, not a finished piece of code itself", "A type of programming language", "A synonym for a software bug"], answer: 1, exp: "Design patterns are conceptual templates, adapted to the specifics of each situation they're applied to." },
  { id: "sep-37", topic: "se-principles", q: "The Spiral model's iterative risk-driven approach makes it particularly suitable for:", options: ["Tiny, trivial, no-risk projects only", "Large, complex, high-risk projects where requirements may be unclear and risks need continuous management", "Projects with absolutely no budget for risk analysis", "Projects that never change over time"], answer: 1, exp: "The Spiral model's repeated risk-assessment cycles are especially valuable when uncertainty and risk are high." },
  { id: "sep-38", topic: "se-principles", q: "A key criticism of the V-Model is:", options: ["It has no testing phases at all", "Like Waterfall, it can be inflexible to changing requirements since testing is planned early but actual execution still largely follows a sequential flow", "It cannot be used for safety-critical systems", "It requires zero documentation"], answer: 1, exp: "The V-Model's early test planning is a strength, but its underlying sequential structure shares Waterfall's rigidity." },
  { id: "sep-39", topic: "se-principles", q: "Software maintenance typically consumes:", options: ["A negligible share of a software system's total lifecycle cost", "A very large share (often the majority) of a software system's total lifecycle cost, more than the initial development", "Exactly the same cost as initial development, always", "No cost at all after deployment"], answer: 1, exp: "This is a widely cited finding in software engineering — maintenance often dominates total cost of ownership." },
  { id: "sep-40", topic: "se-principles", q: "A well-modularized system with low coupling benefits maintenance because:", options: ["Changes to one module are more likely to unintentionally break other modules", "Changes to one module are less likely to unintentionally break other, loosely-connected modules", "Modularity has no effect on maintenance effort", "Low coupling always increases bugs"], answer: 1, exp: "Loosely coupled modules can generally be changed with less risk of unexpected side effects elsewhere." },
  { id: "sep-41", topic: "se-principles", q: "An SRS document being 'traceable' means:", options: ["It cannot be referenced by any other document", "Each requirement can be tracked through design, implementation, and testing, to confirm it has been properly addressed", "It is written in an untraceable, informal style", "It only applies to hardware requirements"], answer: 1, exp: "Traceability links requirements to the design/code/tests that implement and verify them." },
  { id: "sep-42", topic: "se-principles", q: "A prototype used purely to clarify requirements and then discarded (not evolved into the final product) is called a:", options: ["Evolutionary prototype", "Throwaway (rapid) prototype", "Final production system", "Waterfall deliverable"], answer: 1, exp: "Throwaway prototypes exist solely to explore/clarify requirements, then are set aside once their purpose is served." },
  { id: "sep-43", topic: "se-principles", q: "An evolutionary prototype, unlike a throwaway prototype, is:", options: ["Always discarded immediately after use", "Iteratively refined and ultimately evolves into the final delivered system, rather than being discarded", "Never shown to any stakeholder", "Built with no intention of ever being used"], answer: 1, exp: "Evolutionary prototyping treats the prototype as the seed of the eventual production system." },
  { id: "sep-44", topic: "se-principles", q: "The primary reason software engineering emerged as a distinct discipline (rather than just 'programming') was:", options: ["Programming languages became too simple", "The growing complexity, scale, and cost of software projects, which needed systematic engineering practices (the so-called 'software crisis')", "Hardware became less important over time", "Software projects stopped needing any planning"], answer: 1, exp: "The 'software crisis' of large, late, over-budget, unreliable projects motivated a more disciplined engineering approach." },
  { id: "sep-45", topic: "se-principles", q: "A stakeholder in a software project refers to:", options: ["Only the lead developer", "Anyone with an interest in or who is affected by the project's outcome, including users, clients, developers, and management", "Only the company's shareholders", "Only people who wrote the requirements document"], answer: 1, exp: "Stakeholders span a broad group whose needs and concerns matter to project success." },
  { id: "sep-46", topic: "se-principles", q: "Choosing an appropriate SDLC model for a project should primarily depend on:", options: ["Always picking Waterfall regardless of project type", "Factors like requirement stability, project size/complexity, risk level, and timeline, rather than a single model always being 'best'", "Random selection with no analysis", "Whichever model is most fashionable that year"], answer: 1, exp: "No single SDLC model fits every project; the right choice depends on the project's specific characteristics." },
  { id: "sep-47", topic: "se-principles", q: "A key output artifact of the design phase in the SDLC is typically:", options: ["The final signed contract with the client", "A design document/specification describing the system's architecture and detailed component designs, to guide implementation", "The user acceptance test results", "The deployed production environment"], answer: 1, exp: "This design artifact bridges the gap between requirements and actual implementation." },
  { id: "sep-48", topic: "se-principles", q: "'Scope creep' in a software project refers to:", options: ["A project finishing exactly as originally planned", "Uncontrolled expansion of the project's requirements/features beyond what was originally agreed, often without corresponding adjustments to schedule/budget", "A deliberate, well-managed change request process", "Reducing a project's scope to save time"], answer: 1, exp: "Scope creep is a common risk to project schedules and budgets when new requirements are added informally." },
  { id: "sep-49", topic: "se-principles", q: "A key advantage of the Incremental model over pure Waterfall is:", options: ["It never delivers anything until the very end, just like Waterfall", "Early increments deliver usable, partial functionality sooner, providing earlier value and feedback than waiting for the entire system to be complete", "It requires no planning whatsoever", "It cannot be combined with any testing"], answer: 1, exp: "Delivering working increments early lets stakeholders start getting value and giving feedback sooner." },
  { id: "sep-50", topic: "se-principles", q: "Overall, software engineering principles and SDLC models exist to:", options: ["Make software development entirely unpredictable", "Bring discipline, predictability, and quality to the process of building software, managing the inherent complexity and risk of large software projects", "Eliminate the need for any documentation or planning", "Guarantee every project finishes early and under budget with no effort"], answer: 1, exp: "These principles/models are tools for managing complexity and risk, not guarantees, but they substantially improve outcomes when applied well." },

  // ================= Agile Software Development (50) =================
  { id: "ag-01", topic: "se-agile", q: "The Agile Manifesto values:", options: ["Processes and tools over individuals and interactions", "Individuals and interactions over processes and tools, working software over comprehensive documentation, customer collaboration over contract negotiation, and responding to change over following a plan", "Comprehensive documentation over working software", "Following a plan over responding to change"], answer: 1, exp: "These four value statements are the foundation of the Agile Manifesto (while still valuing the items on the right, just less than the items on the left)." },
  { id: "ag-02", topic: "se-agile", q: "Agile software development, compared to traditional Waterfall, emphasizes:", options: ["A single fixed upfront plan with no changes allowed", "Iterative, incremental delivery with frequent feedback and adaptability to change", "Avoiding any customer involvement until the final delivery", "Eliminating all documentation permanently"], answer: 1, exp: "Agile's core distinguishing trait is embracing change through short, feedback-driven cycles." },
  { id: "ag-03", topic: "se-agile", q: "In Scrum, the Product Owner's primary responsibility is to:", options: ["Write all the code personally", "Represent stakeholder/customer interests, prioritize the Product Backlog, and define what should be built", "Facilitate daily standups only", "Manage the team's HR issues"], answer: 1, exp: "The Product Owner owns the 'what' and 'why' of the product, prioritizing value for stakeholders." },
  { id: "ag-04", topic: "se-agile", q: "In Scrum, the Scrum Master's primary responsibility is to:", options: ["Assign specific coding tasks to each developer directly", "Facilitate the Scrum process, remove impediments/blockers, and help the team follow Scrum practices, rather than directing the team's technical work", "Own the Product Backlog", "Approve the final release to production alone"], answer: 1, exp: "The Scrum Master serves the team and process, not command-and-control management." },
  { id: "ag-05", topic: "se-agile", q: "In Scrum, the Development Team is responsible for:", options: ["Only writing requirements documents", "Self-organizing to deliver a working increment of the product each Sprint", "Only attending meetings with no actual delivery", "Reporting directly to external stakeholders instead of the Product Owner"], answer: 1, exp: "The Development Team owns the 'how' — figuring out and executing the work to deliver each Sprint's increment." },
  { id: "ag-06", topic: "se-agile", q: "A Sprint in Scrum is:", options: ["An unbounded period with no fixed length", "A fixed, time-boxed period (commonly 1-4 weeks) during which a specific set of work is completed and made ready for review", "Always exactly one year long", "A meeting held only once per project"], answer: 1, exp: "The fixed time-box is a defining structural feature of Scrum." },
  { id: "ag-07", topic: "se-agile", q: "The Product Backlog in Scrum is:", options: ["A fixed, never-changing list finalized before the project starts", "A prioritized, evolving list of all desired features/requirements for the product, maintained by the Product Owner", "A list only of completed work", "A list of team members' vacation days"], answer: 1, exp: "The Product Backlog is a living artifact, continuously refined and reprioritized." },
  { id: "ag-08", topic: "se-agile", q: "The Sprint Backlog in Scrum is:", options: ["Identical to the entire Product Backlog", "The subset of Product Backlog items selected for the current Sprint, along with the plan for delivering them", "A list of bugs found after release", "Only used by the Scrum Master, never the team"], answer: 1, exp: "The Sprint Backlog scopes and plans just the current Sprint's work." },
  { id: "ag-09", topic: "se-agile", q: "Sprint Planning, a Scrum event, is used to:", options: ["Review completed work with stakeholders", "Determine what work will be done in the upcoming Sprint and how the team plans to accomplish it", "Reflect on the team's own process improvements", "Only occur once at the very start of the entire project"], answer: 1, exp: "Sprint Planning kicks off each Sprint by selecting and planning its scope of work." },
  { id: "ag-10", topic: "se-agile", q: "The Daily Scrum (daily standup) is a short meeting primarily used to:", options: ["Conduct a full Sprint Review", "Synchronize the team on progress, plans for the day, and any impediments, typically time-boxed to about 15 minutes", "Replace the need for any other Scrum events", "Only happen once per Sprint"], answer: 1, exp: "The Daily Scrum is a brief, frequent synchronization point, not a status report to management." },
  { id: "ag-11", topic: "se-agile", q: "The Sprint Review, a Scrum event, is held to:", options: ["Only discuss the team's internal process", "Demonstrate the completed increment to stakeholders and gather feedback at the end of a Sprint", "Plan the next Sprint's backlog exclusively", "Fire underperforming team members"], answer: 1, exp: "The Sprint Review is outward-facing, showcasing the increment and collecting stakeholder input." },
  { id: "ag-12", topic: "se-agile", q: "The Sprint Retrospective, a Scrum event, is held to:", options: ["Demonstrate the product to external stakeholders", "Reflect on the team's own process and identify improvements for how they work together going forward", "Plan the next release's marketing", "Only occur at the very end of the entire project"], answer: 1, exp: "The Retrospective is inward-facing, focused on continuous team process improvement." },
  { id: "ag-13", topic: "se-agile", q: "A user story in Agile development is typically written in the format:", options: ["A formal legal contract clause", "'As a [role], I want [feature], so that [benefit]' — capturing a requirement from the user's perspective", "A raw technical specification with no user context", "A single word describing the feature"], answer: 1, exp: "This template keeps requirements framed around user value and motivation." },
  { id: "ag-14", topic: "se-agile", q: "Story points in Agile estimation represent:", options: ["A direct measure of hours or days", "A relative measure of effort/complexity/uncertainty for completing a piece of work, rather than a direct measure of time", "A count of the number of bugs in a feature", "A financial cost estimate in currency"], answer: 1, exp: "Story points deliberately avoid direct time units, focusing on relative sizing instead." },
  { id: "ag-15", topic: "se-agile", q: "Velocity in Scrum refers to:", options: ["The speed of the team's internet connection", "The amount of work (e.g., story points) a team typically completes per Sprint, used to help forecast future Sprint capacity", "The number of meetings held per week", "The physical speed of code execution"], answer: 1, exp: "Velocity is a team-specific historical throughput metric used for planning, not a universal comparison metric across teams." },
  { id: "ag-16", topic: "se-agile", q: "A burndown chart in Agile project tracking shows:", options: ["The total revenue generated by the product", "Remaining work (e.g., story points or tasks) over time within a Sprint (or release), helping visualize progress toward completion", "The number of team members hired over time", "The app's server uptime"], answer: 1, exp: "Burndown charts track how much work is left, trending toward zero as the Sprint/release progresses." },
  { id: "ag-17", topic: "se-agile", q: "Kanban, as an Agile-related method, is centered around:", options: ["Fixed-length Sprints with mandatory roles", "Visualizing work on a board and limiting Work In Progress (WIP) to improve flow, rather than fixed-length iterations like Scrum's Sprints", "Eliminating all visual tracking of work", "Requiring a Product Owner and Scrum Master by name"], answer: 1, exp: "Kanban emphasizes continuous flow and WIP limits over Scrum's time-boxed iteration structure." },
  { id: "ag-18", topic: "se-agile", q: "A Kanban board typically organizes work into columns such as:", options: ["Only a single undivided column", "'To Do', 'In Progress', and 'Done', visually representing each item's current status", "Only 'Success' and 'Failure'", "Columns representing team members' salaries"], answer: 1, exp: "Column-based status tracking is the visual core of the Kanban method." },
  { id: "ag-19", topic: "se-agile", q: "A WIP (Work In Progress) limit on a Kanban board is used to:", options: ["Encourage starting as many tasks as possible simultaneously", "Cap how many items can be in a given stage simultaneously, helping surface bottlenecks and encouraging the team to finish work before starting more", "Remove the need for a Kanban board entirely", "Only apply to the 'Done' column"], answer: 1, exp: "WIP limits are Kanban's core mechanism for improving flow and surfacing process bottlenecks." },
  { id: "ag-20", topic: "se-agile", q: "A key difference between Scrum and Kanban is that Scrum uses:", options: ["Continuous flow with no defined roles, unlike Kanban", "Fixed-length Sprints with defined roles/ceremonies, while Kanban emphasizes continuous flow without mandatory time-boxed iterations", "Exactly the same structure as Kanban with no differences", "No backlog at all, unlike Kanban"], answer: 1, exp: "This structural distinction (time-boxed vs. continuous flow) is the key differentiator between the two methods." },
  { id: "ag-21", topic: "se-agile", q: "Extreme Programming (XP) is an Agile methodology particularly known for practices like:", options: ["Working entirely alone with no code review", "Pair programming, test-driven development (TDD), and continuous integration", "Avoiding all forms of testing", "Releasing software only once per year"], answer: 1, exp: "These engineering-focused practices distinguish XP from more process-focused frameworks like Scrum." },
  { id: "ag-22", topic: "se-agile", q: "Pair programming, an XP practice, involves:", options: ["Two developers working on entirely separate, unrelated tasks", "Two developers working together at one workstation, one writing code ('driver') while the other reviews/thinks ahead ('navigator')", "A developer pairing with a project manager for scheduling", "Automated code generation with no human involvement"], answer: 1, exp: "This real-time collaborative coding pattern is central to XP's approach to quality and knowledge sharing." },
  { id: "ag-23", topic: "se-agile", q: "Test-Driven Development (TDD), an XP practice, follows the cycle:", options: ["Write code first, then decide whether to test it later", "Write a failing test first, then write just enough code to make it pass, then refactor", "Only write tests after the entire project is finished", "Skip testing entirely and rely on manual QA"], answer: 1, exp: "This 'red-green-refactor' cycle is the core discipline of TDD." },
  { id: "ag-24", topic: "se-agile", q: "Continuous Integration (CI), an XP/Agile practice, involves:", options: ["Merging code changes only once per year", "Frequently merging code changes into a shared repository and automatically building/testing them, to catch integration issues early", "Avoiding automated builds entirely", "Only integrating code manually with no automation"], answer: 1, exp: "Frequent integration with automated verification catches problems while they're still small and easy to fix." },
  { id: "ag-25", topic: "se-agile", q: "Refactoring, an XP practice, means:", options: ["Adding new features to the code", "Improving a code's internal structure/design without changing its external behavior", "Deleting all existing code and starting over", "Writing code with no tests"], answer: 1, exp: "Refactoring is specifically about improving internal quality while preserving observable behavior." },
  { id: "ag-26", topic: "se-agile", q: "XP's practice of frequent small releases aims to:", options: ["Delay all feedback until the very end of the project", "Deliver working software to users/customers often, gathering feedback and reducing the risk of large, infrequent releases", "Release software only once, at project completion", "Avoid ever involving customers in the release process"], answer: 1, exp: "Small, frequent releases reduce risk and speed up the feedback loop with real users." },
  { id: "ag-27", topic: "se-agile", q: "A self-organizing team in Agile means:", options: ["The team is told exactly how to do every task by external management", "The team itself decides how best to accomplish its work, rather than being told exactly how by external management", "There is no team structure at all", "Only the Scrum Master makes all technical decisions"], answer: 1, exp: "Self-organization is a core Agile principle, trusting the team's expertise in HOW to deliver the work." },
  { id: "ag-28", topic: "se-agile", q: "An MVP (Minimum Viable Product) in Agile/lean product development is:", options: ["The fully-featured final version of the product", "The smallest version of a product with just enough features to be usable and gather validated learning from real users", "A product with zero functionality", "A prototype that is never shown to any user"], answer: 1, exp: "An MVP focuses on maximizing learning from real users with minimal upfront investment." },
  { id: "ag-29", topic: "se-agile", q: "Backlog grooming (refinement) in Scrum is the ongoing activity of:", options: ["Deleting the Product Backlog entirely at the end of each Sprint", "Reviewing, clarifying, estimating, and reprioritizing Product Backlog items to keep them ready for future Sprints", "Only occurring once, at the very start of the project", "Assigning backlog items randomly with no analysis"], answer: 1, exp: "Ongoing refinement keeps the backlog well-understood and ready for upcoming Sprint Planning." },
  { id: "ag-30", topic: "se-agile", q: "A 'Definition of Done' (DoD) in Scrum is:", options: ["A vague, undefined personal opinion of each developer", "A shared, agreed-upon checklist/criteria that a Product Backlog item must meet to be considered truly complete", "Only relevant to the Product Owner, not the team", "Something that changes randomly every Sprint with no consistency"], answer: 1, exp: "A shared DoD ensures everyone has a consistent understanding of what 'complete' actually means." },
  { id: "ag-31", topic: "se-agile", q: "A key benefit of Agile's iterative delivery approach is:", options: ["Delaying all stakeholder feedback until the project's very end", "Earlier and more frequent opportunities to gather stakeholder feedback and adjust direction, reducing the risk of building the wrong thing", "Guaranteeing the initial plan never needs to change", "Eliminating the need for any stakeholder involvement"], answer: 1, exp: "Frequent feedback loops are central to Agile's ability to course-correct early." },
  { id: "ag-32", topic: "se-agile", q: "A common criticism/challenge of Agile methods is that:", options: ["They are always easier to apply with fixed, rigid upfront contracts", "They can be harder to apply with fixed, rigid upfront contracts/budgets that assume a fully-specified scope from the start", "They eliminate the need for any planning whatsoever", "They cannot be used by any software team"], answer: 1, exp: "Agile's embrace of changing scope can conflict with contract structures that assume a fixed, upfront specification." },
  { id: "ag-33", topic: "se-agile", q: "In Scrum, an 'Increment' refers to:", options: ["The sum of all completed Product Backlog items at the end of a Sprint, forming a potentially shippable piece of working software", "A single line of code", "A meeting held to discuss increments in team salary", "The Product Backlog itself"], answer: 0, exp: "The Increment is the tangible, potentially-releasable result of a Sprint's work." },
  { id: "ag-34", topic: "se-agile", q: "Agile's emphasis on 'working software over comprehensive documentation' means:", options: ["No documentation is ever written under any circumstances", "Documentation is de-prioritized relative to Waterfall, in favor of prioritizing delivering functional software, though some documentation is still valuable", "Documentation is considered more important than working software", "Documentation and working software are considered identical concepts"], answer: 1, exp: "The Agile Manifesto values working software MORE than documentation, not documentation NOT AT ALL." },
  { id: "ag-35", topic: "se-agile", q: "A 'Scrum of Scrums' is used when:", options: ["A single small team needs internal coordination", "Coordinating multiple Scrum teams working on a larger, related product, syncing progress and dependencies across teams", "There is only one developer on the entire project", "The Product Owner needs to fire the Scrum Master"], answer: 1, exp: "Scrum of Scrums scales coordination across multiple teams working toward a shared larger goal." },
  { id: "ag-36", topic: "se-agile", q: "An epic, in Agile backlog terminology, is:", options: ["A tiny task that takes minutes to complete", "A large body of work/requirement that is too big for a single Sprint and is typically broken down into smaller user stories", "A synonym for a bug report", "A meeting held only at project kickoff"], answer: 1, exp: "Epics are large themes of work later decomposed into Sprint-sized user stories." },
  { id: "ag-37", topic: "se-agile", q: "A key reason time-boxing (fixed Sprint lengths) is used in Scrum is to:", options: ["Make the schedule as unpredictable as possible", "Create a regular, predictable cadence for planning, review, and feedback, helping maintain steady progress", "Ensure every Sprint has a different, random length", "Eliminate the need for any Sprint Review"], answer: 1, exp: "Consistent Sprint lengths establish a reliable rhythm for the team and stakeholders." },
  { id: "ag-38", topic: "se-agile", q: "'Sprint zero', sometimes used informally at a project's start, refers to:", options: ["The final Sprint before project cancellation", "An initial preparatory period for setup, initial planning, and backlog creation before regular development Sprints begin", "A Sprint with negative velocity", "A mandatory formal part of the official Scrum Guide"], answer: 1, exp: "Sprint zero is a common informal practice, not an official Scrum artifact, used to prepare before regular Sprints start." },
  { id: "ag-39", topic: "se-agile", q: "In Agile estimation, planning poker is a technique where:", options: ["Team members openly discuss estimates one at a time in sequence, anchoring on the first number given", "Team members privately and simultaneously estimate a story's size (often using cards with values), then discuss and converge on an estimate, avoiding anchoring bias", "Only the Scrum Master decides all estimates alone", "Estimates are decided by a random dice roll with no discussion"], answer: 1, exp: "Simultaneous, private estimation followed by discussion helps avoid one person's early guess unduly influencing others." },
  { id: "ag-40", topic: "se-agile", q: "A cross-functional Agile team typically includes:", options: ["Only developers, with no other skill sets represented", "Members with all the skills needed to deliver a working increment (e.g., development, testing, design) without depending heavily on outside teams", "Only project managers, with no technical staff", "Team members from unrelated, entirely different products"], answer: 1, exp: "Cross-functional teams minimize external dependencies, letting the team deliver end-to-end value independently." },
  { id: "ag-41", topic: "se-agile", q: "Continuous Delivery, closely related to Agile/DevOps practices, means:", options: ["Deploying to production manually once a year", "Keeping the software in a state that could be released to production at any time, following automated build/test pipelines", "Never automating any part of the build or deployment process", "Deleting all previous releases after each deployment"], answer: 1, exp: "Continuous Delivery keeps a release candidate always ready, even if the actual release decision is made separately." },
  { id: "ag-42", topic: "se-agile", q: "A key motivation for Agile's frequent stakeholder feedback loops is:", options: ["Increasing the risk of building unwanted features", "Reducing the risk of investing significant effort building features that turn out not to meet actual user/business needs", "Eliminating the need for any stakeholder communication", "Guaranteeing the product needs no further changes after release"], answer: 1, exp: "Frequent feedback catches misalignment between what's built and what's actually needed, earlier rather than later." },
  { id: "ag-43", topic: "se-agile", q: "Compared to Waterfall's single large delivery at project end, Agile's incremental delivery:", options: ["Only provides value at the very end, exactly like Waterfall", "Provides value to users/stakeholders earlier and more often, throughout the project rather than only at the very end", "Never provides any usable functionality at all", "Requires waiting even longer than Waterfall to see any results"], answer: 1, exp: "This earlier, more frequent value delivery is one of Agile's central practical advantages." },
  { id: "ag-44", topic: "se-agile", q: "A 'spike' in Agile development refers to:", options: ["A sudden, uncontrolled increase in project budget", "A time-boxed research/investigation task used to explore a technical unknown or reduce uncertainty before committing to an approach", "A permanent feature added without any planning", "A synonym for a Sprint Retrospective"], answer: 1, exp: "Spikes let teams investigate unknowns in a bounded, controlled way before fully committing engineering effort." },
  { id: "ag-45", topic: "se-agile", q: "Servant leadership, a concept often associated with the Scrum Master role, means:", options: ["Commanding the team's every technical decision", "Leading by supporting and removing obstacles for the team, rather than directing/commanding their work", "Ignoring the team's needs entirely", "Only serving the interests of upper management"], answer: 1, exp: "Servant leadership focuses on enabling the team's success rather than exercising top-down authority." },
  { id: "ag-46", topic: "se-agile", q: "A team consistently failing to complete all committed Sprint Backlog items might indicate:", options: ["The team is perfectly calibrated and needs no adjustment", "The team is over-committing relative to its actual velocity/capacity, suggesting a need to recalibrate future Sprint planning", "The Product Owner should be immediately replaced", "Sprint length should be extended to one full year"], answer: 1, exp: "Consistently missed commitments are a signal to use actual velocity data to plan more realistically going forward." },
  { id: "ag-47", topic: "se-agile", q: "Agile retrospectives following the principle of continuous improvement primarily aim to:", options: ["Assign blame to individual team members", "Help the team incrementally get better at how they work together over successive Sprints", "Only be held once, at the project's very end", "Replace the need for Sprint Planning"], answer: 1, exp: "Retrospectives are a structured, recurring mechanism for incremental team process improvement." },
  { id: "ag-48", topic: "se-agile", q: "A 'user story' being broken down into smaller stories when it's too large for one Sprint follows the Agile principle of:", options: ["Making every story as large and complex as possible", "Keeping work items small enough to be completed, tested, and delivered within a single iteration", "Never splitting any story under any circumstances", "Only splitting stories after the Sprint has already ended"], answer: 1, exp: "Right-sized stories that fit within a Sprint are essential to Scrum's iterative delivery rhythm." },
  { id: "ag-49", topic: "se-agile", q: "One reason Agile methods often suit projects with unclear or evolving requirements better than Waterfall is:", options: ["Agile requires all requirements to be fixed permanently at the start, just like Waterfall", "Agile's iterative cycles allow requirements to be refined and re-prioritized based on ongoing feedback, rather than needing to be fully fixed upfront", "Agile eliminates the need for any requirements at all", "Waterfall is always faster regardless of requirement clarity"], answer: 1, exp: "This adaptability to evolving requirements is a central advantage of Agile over rigid, plan-driven approaches." },
  { id: "ag-50", topic: "se-agile", q: "Overall, Agile methodologies (Scrum, Kanban, XP and others) share a common goal of:", options: ["Rigid, unchanging upfront planning with no feedback", "Delivering value incrementally and adapting to change through short feedback cycles, collaboration, and iterative development, rather than rigid upfront planning", "Eliminating any form of collaboration between team members", "Guaranteeing zero changes throughout the entire project"], answer: 1, exp: "This shared underlying philosophy — incremental delivery, adaptability, and collaboration — unites the various Agile frameworks and practices." },

  // ================= Software Testing & Project Management (50) =================
  { id: "tp-01", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Unit testing verifies:", options: ["The entire integrated system at once", "The smallest individually testable pieces of code (e.g., functions/methods) in isolation", "Only the user interface", "Only the deployment infrastructure"], answer: 1, exp: "Unit tests isolate the smallest testable units, making failures easy to localize." },
  { id: "tp-02", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Integration testing verifies:", options: ["Only a single function in isolation", "That different modules/components work correctly together when combined, catching interface/interaction issues", "Only the visual design of the app", "Only the project's budget"], answer: 1, exp: "Integration testing focuses on the interactions between already-unit-tested components." },
  { id: "tp-03", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "System testing verifies:", options: ["Only isolated individual functions", "The complete, integrated system as a whole against its specified requirements", "Only a single module's internal logic", "Only the project's marketing plan"], answer: 1, exp: "System testing evaluates the whole application end-to-end against its requirements." },
  { id: "tp-04", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Acceptance testing verifies:", options: ["Only internal code structure", "That the system meets the customer's/user's actual needs and is ready for delivery, often performed by or with the end users/client", "Only the developer's personal preferences", "Only unit-level correctness"], answer: 1, exp: "Acceptance testing is the final validation that the system is genuinely fit for its intended use." },
  { id: "tp-05", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Black-box testing evaluates software by:", options: ["Examining internal source code line by line", "Testing functionality against inputs and expected outputs without knowledge of the internal code structure/implementation", "Only testing the database schema", "Requiring full access to the compiled binary's assembly code"], answer: 1, exp: "Black-box testing treats the system as an opaque box, focusing purely on input/output behavior." },
  { id: "tp-06", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "White-box testing evaluates software by:", options: ["Ignoring the code entirely and only checking outputs", "Examining and testing the internal code structure/logic directly (e.g., testing specific code paths), requiring knowledge of the implementation", "Only testing from the end user's perspective with no code knowledge", "Only applicable to hardware testing"], answer: 1, exp: "White-box testing requires visibility into the code to design tests exercising specific internal paths/branches." },
  { id: "tp-07", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Grey-box testing is:", options: ["Identical to pure white-box testing", "A combination approach, where the tester has partial knowledge of the internal implementation while still testing largely from a black-box (functional) perspective", "A testing approach that never involves any functional checks", "Only used for hardware, never software"], answer: 1, exp: "Grey-box testing blends partial internal knowledge with an overall functional testing perspective." },
  { id: "tp-08", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Regression testing is performed to:", options: ["Test entirely new, never-before-tested functionality only", "Verify that recent code changes haven't broken previously working functionality", "Replace the need for any other type of testing", "Only run once, at the very start of a project"], answer: 1, exp: "Regression testing guards against previously working features breaking due to new changes." },
  { id: "tp-09", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Smoke testing (a quick, shallow test pass) is used to:", options: ["Perform a full, exhaustive test of every feature", "Quickly verify that the most critical functionality works at a basic level before committing to more thorough testing", "Replace regression testing entirely", "Only be run after the software has been fully deprecated"], answer: 1, exp: "Smoke tests are a fast initial sanity check before investing in deeper testing." },
  { id: "tp-10", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Sanity testing, closely related to smoke testing, is used to:", options: ["Perform a comprehensive full regression pass", "Quickly check that a specific bug fix or new functionality works as intended, without doing a full regression pass", "Only be used at the very end of a project's lifecycle", "Replace the need for any code review"], answer: 1, exp: "Sanity testing is a narrow, focused check on a specific recent change." },
  { id: "tp-11", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Alpha testing is typically performed:", options: ["By anonymous external users with no company involvement", "Internally, by the development organization's own testers/staff, before the software is released to external users", "Only after the public release", "By a random sample of the general public"], answer: 1, exp: "Alpha testing happens in-house, prior to any external exposure." },
  { id: "tp-12", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Beta testing is typically performed:", options: ["Only internally by developers", "By a limited group of real external users in a real-world environment, before the software's full public release", "Before any code has even been written", "Only after the software has already reached end-of-life"], answer: 1, exp: "Beta testing gathers real-world feedback from actual (though limited) external users before full launch." },
  { id: "tp-13", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A test case in software testing consists of:", options: ["Only a vague description with no specifics", "A set of preconditions, input values, execution steps, and expected results used to verify a specific behavior", "Only the final production release notes", "A random guess at what might work"], answer: 1, exp: "Well-structured test cases are specific and repeatable, enabling consistent verification." },
  { id: "tp-14", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Test automation is most beneficial for:", options: ["One-off exploratory checks that will never be repeated", "Repetitive tests (like regression suites) that need to be run frequently, saving significant manual effort over time", "Tasks requiring deep human intuition and judgment", "Usability evaluation requiring subjective human feedback"], answer: 1, exp: "Automation pays off most where tests are run repeatedly, amortizing the upfront scripting investment." },
  { id: "tp-15", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Manual testing remains valuable especially for:", options: ["Highly repetitive regression checks best suited to automation", "Exploratory testing and usability evaluation, where human judgment/intuition is important, unlike scripted automated checks", "Tasks with absolutely no need for human judgment", "Situations where consistency across thousands of repeated runs matters most"], answer: 1, exp: "Manual testing excels where human creativity, intuition, and subjective judgment matter most." },
  { id: "tp-16", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A 'defect' or 'bug' lifecycle typically includes states such as:", options: ["Only 'Exists' and 'Does Not Exist'", "New, Assigned, Open/In Progress, Fixed, Retested, and Closed", "Only 'Reported' with no further tracking", "States that are irrelevant to defect management"], answer: 1, exp: "A defined lifecycle tracks a bug's progress from discovery through resolution and verification." },
  { id: "tp-17", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A 'defect tracking system' (e.g., Jira, Bugzilla) is used to:", options: ["Only display marketing statistics", "Log, track, prioritize, and manage the status of reported software defects throughout their lifecycle", "Replace the need for any actual testing", "Only be used by project managers, never testers or developers"], answer: 1, exp: "Defect tracking tools centralize visibility and management of reported issues across the team." },
  { id: "tp-18", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Functional testing verifies:", options: ["How fast the system performs under load", "That the software performs its specified functions correctly, i.e., WHAT the system does", "Only the system's security posture", "Only the system's visual color scheme"], answer: 1, exp: "Functional testing checks feature correctness against specifications." },
  { id: "tp-19", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Non-functional testing verifies quality attributes such as:", options: ["Only specific features/functions", "Performance, security, usability, or reliability — i.e., HOW WELL the system performs, rather than specific features", "Only the project's budget", "Only the source code's line count"], answer: 1, exp: "Non-functional testing complements functional testing by checking quality attributes rather than specific behaviors." },
  { id: "tp-20", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Performance testing, a type of non-functional testing, evaluates:", options: ["Only whether a specific feature exists", "The system's responsiveness, throughput, and stability under a given workload", "Only the color scheme of the UI", "Only the project's documentation completeness"], answer: 1, exp: "Performance testing measures how well the system performs under realistic operating conditions." },
  { id: "tp-21", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Load testing, a specific type of performance testing, evaluates:", options: ["The system's behavior with zero users", "How the system behaves under an expected (or increasing) level of concurrent user/traffic load", "Only the visual layout of the UI", "Only the correctness of a single function"], answer: 1, exp: "Load testing checks how the system holds up under realistic or growing concurrent demand." },
  { id: "tp-22", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Stress testing, another type of performance testing, evaluates:", options: ["Only normal, expected operating conditions", "How the system behaves when pushed beyond its normal operating capacity, to find its breaking point", "Only the aesthetic quality of the interface", "Only whether documentation is complete"], answer: 1, exp: "Stress testing intentionally pushes past normal limits to observe failure behavior." },
  { id: "tp-23", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Security testing evaluates:", options: ["Only visual design consistency", "Whether the system properly protects data and resources against unauthorized access, vulnerabilities, and attacks", "Only the speed of the system under load", "Only whether the code compiles without errors"], answer: 1, exp: "Security testing specifically probes for vulnerabilities and unauthorized access risks." },
  { id: "tp-24", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Usability testing evaluates:", options: ["Only the system's raw processing speed", "How easily and effectively real users can accomplish their tasks using the system's interface", "Only whether the code has any syntax errors", "Only the system's network bandwidth usage"], answer: 1, exp: "Usability testing centers on the real user's experience accomplishing tasks with the interface." },
  { id: "tp-25", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Quality Assurance (QA), as distinct from Quality Control (QC), primarily focuses on:", options: ["Only finding defects in the finished product", "Process-oriented activities aimed at preventing defects (e.g., defining standards/processes), whereas QC focuses on identifying defects in the actual product", "Exactly the same activities as QC with no distinction", "Only marketing the finished product"], answer: 1, exp: "QA is proactive/process-focused; QC is reactive/product-focused, identifying defects that do occur." },
  { id: "tp-26", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "An SLA (Service Level Agreement) in a software/IT project context is:", options: ["An informal, unwritten understanding with no specifics", "A formal agreement specifying measurable service commitments (e.g., uptime, response time, defect resolution time) between a provider and a customer", "A type of programming language", "Only relevant to hardware manufacturing"], answer: 1, exp: "SLAs formalize measurable service expectations between a provider and its customers." },
  { id: "tp-27", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "An SLA typically includes:", options: ["No measurable targets of any kind", "Specific, measurable targets/metrics and often defined consequences (penalties/credits) if those targets are not met", "Only vague, unquantifiable promises", "Only the provider's marketing slogans"], answer: 1, exp: "Concrete, measurable targets (and consequences) are what make an SLA enforceable and meaningful." },
  { id: "tp-28", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Effort/cost estimation models like COCOMO (Constructive Cost Model) are used in software project management to:", options: ["Only estimate the color scheme of the final UI", "Estimate the effort, cost, and schedule required for a software project, often based on estimated size (e.g., lines of code) and other project factors", "Replace the need for any testing", "Only apply to hardware procurement"], answer: 1, exp: "COCOMO and similar models provide a structured basis for effort/cost/schedule estimation." },
  { id: "tp-29", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A Gantt chart in project management is used to:", options: ["Only display the final financial report", "Visually represent a project's schedule, showing tasks, their durations, and dependencies over time", "Only track individual lines of code written", "Replace the need for any project planning"], answer: 1, exp: "Gantt charts are a classic visual tool for project scheduling and dependency tracking." },
  { id: "tp-30", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Risk management in software project management involves:", options: ["Ignoring all potential problems until they occur", "Identifying, assessing, and planning responses to potential risks that could negatively impact the project's success", "Only occurring after the project has already failed", "Only relevant to hardware-only projects"], answer: 1, exp: "Proactive risk management aims to anticipate and mitigate problems before they derail the project." },
  { id: "tp-31", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A project's 'critical path' (as used in project scheduling, e.g., CPM) refers to:", options: ["Any single, unimportant task in the project", "The sequence of dependent tasks that determines the shortest possible overall project duration; delaying any task on it delays the whole project", "A task that can be delayed indefinitely with no impact on the schedule", "The project's total budget, unrelated to scheduling"], answer: 1, exp: "The critical path directly determines the project's minimum achievable duration." },
  { id: "tp-32", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Resource allocation in project management involves:", options: ["Ignoring available people, time, and budget entirely", "Assigning available people, time, and budget to specific project tasks/activities appropriately", "Only relevant to hardware-based projects", "Only occurring after the project is already complete"], answer: 1, exp: "Effective resource allocation matches available capacity to the project's actual task needs." },
  { id: "tp-33", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A project manager's core responsibilities in a software project typically include:", options: ["Only writing the application's source code personally", "Planning, scheduling, coordinating resources, managing risk, and tracking progress toward project goals", "Only designing the user interface", "Only performing final acceptance testing"], answer: 1, exp: "Project management centers on coordinating people, schedule, risk, and progress, distinct from hands-on technical work." },
  { id: "tp-34", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A QA lead's role, distinct from a project manager's, is typically focused on:", options: ["Managing the overall project budget and schedule", "Overseeing the testing strategy and quality processes to ensure the product meets required quality standards", "Only handling client billing", "Only writing marketing copy"], answer: 1, exp: "The QA lead specializes in quality/testing strategy, while the project manager handles broader project coordination." },
  { id: "tp-35", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Version control systems (e.g., Git) support software project management by:", options: ["Preventing any collaboration between developers", "Tracking changes to source code over time, enabling collaboration, and allowing safe rollback to previous versions", "Only being useful for a single solo developer with no team", "Replacing the need for any testing"], answer: 1, exp: "Version control is foundational infrastructure for collaborative, trackable, reversible software development." },
  { id: "tp-36", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A key reason to write test cases before executing tests (rather than testing ad hoc) is:", options: ["To make testing completely random and unrepeatable", "Ensuring systematic, repeatable coverage of the intended functionality, rather than relying on memory or improvisation", "To avoid ever documenting the testing process", "To guarantee no defects will ever be found"], answer: 1, exp: "Predefined test cases ensure consistent, repeatable, and traceable test coverage." },
  { id: "tp-37", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Test coverage, as a testing metric, measures:", options: ["The total number of developers on the team", "The extent (e.g., percentage of code, requirements, or paths) that has actually been exercised by the test suite", "Only the project's total budget", "Only the number of meetings held"], answer: 1, exp: "Test coverage quantifies how much of the system has actually been exercised by testing." },
  { id: "tp-38", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A 'false positive' in testing refers to:", options: ["A test correctly reporting a real defect", "A test reporting a failure/defect when the system actually behaves correctly (an incorrect failure report)", "A test that is never run", "A defect that has already been fixed"], answer: 1, exp: "False positives waste investigation time on issues that don't actually exist." },
  { id: "tp-39", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A 'false negative' in testing refers to:", options: ["A test correctly reporting the system is broken", "A test reporting success/pass when the system actually has a defect (a missed real problem)", "A defect that was never actually present", "A test that always fails regardless of the code"], answer: 1, exp: "False negatives are especially dangerous since they let real defects slip through undetected." },
  { id: "tp-40", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Continuous testing, integrated into a CI/CD pipeline, means:", options: ["Tests are run only once, at the very end of the project", "Automated tests run frequently (e.g., on every code commit) to catch issues as early as possible in the development process", "Testing is performed manually with no automation at all", "Testing is skipped entirely to save time"], answer: 1, exp: "Continuous testing catches problems close to when they're introduced, when they're cheapest to fix." },
  { id: "tp-41", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "An SLA's 'penalty clause' (service credit) exists to:", options: ["Reward the provider for missing service targets", "Provide the customer some compensation/recourse and create accountability if the provider fails to meet agreed service levels", "Have no practical effect on either party", "Only apply if the customer fails to pay on time"], answer: 1, exp: "Penalty clauses give real teeth to SLA commitments, incentivizing the provider to meet them." },
  { id: "tp-42", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Estimating a software project's schedule using expert judgment, historical data (analogy-based), and formal models (like COCOMO) together is generally more reliable than:", options: ["Combining multiple independent estimation approaches", "Relying on a single estimation technique alone, since combining approaches helps cross-check assumptions", "Never estimating the schedule at all", "Only guessing randomly with no data"], answer: 1, exp: "Triangulating across multiple estimation methods helps catch blind spots in any single approach." },
  { id: "tp-43", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A key reason project managers track 'scope, schedule, and budget' together is:", options: ["These three constraints are completely unrelated to each other", "These three constraints are interrelated (the 'iron triangle'); changing one (e.g., adding scope) typically affects the others (schedule/budget)", "Only scope matters; schedule and budget are irrelevant", "Tracking them together has no practical value"], answer: 1, exp: "The 'iron triangle' captures how scope, schedule, and budget/resources are fundamentally interconnected." },
  { id: "tp-44", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Acceptance criteria for a user story/requirement primarily serve to:", options: ["Make the requirement deliberately vague and untestable", "Define specific, testable conditions that must be met for that piece of work to be considered acceptable/complete", "Only be written after the work is already finished", "Replace the need for any testing whatsoever"], answer: 1, exp: "Clear acceptance criteria give both developers and testers a shared, testable definition of 'done' for that item." },
  { id: "tp-45", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A 'test plan' document typically outlines:", options: ["Only the final list of bugs found", "The overall testing strategy, scope, resources, schedule, and approach for testing a project, before detailed test cases are written", "Only the marketing plan for the software", "Only the source code's architecture"], answer: 1, exp: "A test plan sets the overall testing approach and scope before diving into individual test case details." },
  { id: "tp-46", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Exploratory testing differs from scripted testing in that exploratory testing:", options: ["Strictly follows a fixed, predefined script with no deviation", "Is conducted with simultaneous learning, test design and execution, relying on tester's judgment rather than following predefined, fixed test scripts", "Can only be performed by fully automated tools", "Never uncovers any new information about the system"], answer: 1, exp: "Exploratory testing leans on tester creativity and judgment in real time, rather than a rigid predefined script." },
  { id: "tp-47", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A key challenge in defining a good SLA is:", options: ["SLAs are always trivially easy to define with no ambiguity", "Setting targets that are realistic and measurable, since overly strict or vague terms can lead to disputes or unachievable expectations", "SLAs never need to be measurable at all", "SLAs cannot include any penalty clauses"], answer: 1, exp: "Balancing achievability and meaningfulness is a genuine challenge in crafting effective SLA terms." },
  { id: "tp-48", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Defect severity and defect priority, though related, differ in that severity indicates:", options: ["The order/urgency in which a defect should be fixed relative to other work", "The technical impact/seriousness of a defect on the system, while priority indicates the order/urgency in which it should be fixed relative to other work", "Exactly the same thing as priority, with no distinction", "Only the cosmetic appearance of a defect"], answer: 1, exp: "Severity (technical impact) and priority (fix urgency) are related but distinct dimensions of a defect." },
  { id: "tp-49", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "A key benefit of early and continuous testing throughout the SDLC (rather than only at the end) is:", options: ["Defects become more expensive to fix the earlier they're caught", "Catching defects earlier when they are generally cheaper and easier to fix than after the system is largely complete", "Testing has no relationship to when defects are found", "Early testing guarantees the project will need no further testing later"], answer: 1, exp: "This aligns with the broader software engineering principle that the cost of fixing a defect grows the later it's found." },
  { id: "tp-50", topic: "se-testing-pm", q: "Overall, effective software testing combined with disciplined project management (including clear SLAs) helps ensure:", options: ["Only that the project finishes as slowly as possible", "The delivered software meets quality expectations, while the project itself is delivered on time, within budget, and against agreed service commitments", "Neither quality nor schedule are ever considered together", "Testing and project management are entirely unrelated disciplines"], answer: 1, exp: "Together, testing and project management address both the quality of what's delivered and how reliably it's delivered." },

  // ================= SCADA & Software Quality (50) =================
  { id: "sq-01", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "SCADA stands for:", options: ["Software Configuration And Data Analysis", "Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition, a system architecture used to monitor and control industrial processes", "Secure Cloud Application Deployment Architecture", "Standard Control And Design Automation"], answer: 1, exp: "SCADA systems are widely used to monitor/control industrial and infrastructure processes remotely." },
  { id: "sq-02", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "SCADA systems are commonly used in industries such as:", options: ["Only social media platforms", "Power generation/distribution, water treatment, oil and gas, and manufacturing, to remotely monitor and control physical processes", "Only mobile app development", "Only video game development"], answer: 1, exp: "SCADA is foundational to critical infrastructure and industrial automation across many sectors." },
  { id: "sq-03", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "An RTU (Remote Terminal Unit) in a SCADA system is responsible for:", options: ["Only displaying the operator dashboard", "Collecting data from field sensors/equipment and transmitting it to the central monitoring system, and relaying control commands back to field devices", "Only billing customers for electricity usage", "Only compiling application source code"], answer: 1, exp: "RTUs are the field-level data collection and control-relay points in a SCADA architecture." },
  { id: "sq-04", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A PLC (Programmable Logic Controller) in an industrial control context is:", options: ["A type of office desktop computer", "A ruggedized industrial computer used to automate specific control processes (e.g., machinery), often integrated with SCADA systems", "A type of database index", "A social media platform component"], answer: 1, exp: "PLCs are hardened industrial controllers often integrated as field devices within a larger SCADA system." },
  { id: "sq-05", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "An MTU (Master Terminal Unit) or central SCADA server is responsible for:", options: ["Only manufacturing physical sensors", "Collecting data from multiple RTUs/field devices, and providing overall supervisory monitoring and control", "Only handling customer support tickets", "Only rendering the mobile app's UI"], answer: 1, exp: "The MTU/central server aggregates data across many RTUs, providing the overall supervisory view." },
  { id: "sq-06", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "An HMI (Human-Machine Interface) in a SCADA system provides:", options: ["Only the physical wiring between sensors", "The graphical interface through which human operators monitor system status and issue control commands", "Only the compiled machine code of the control software", "Only the network cabling layout"], answer: 1, exp: "The HMI is the operator-facing dashboard for monitoring and controlling the industrial process." },
  { id: "sq-07", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A key security concern specific to many SCADA systems is:", options: ["They are always perfectly secure by design with no vulnerabilities", "Reliance on legacy protocols and hardware not originally designed with modern cybersecurity threats in mind, making them vulnerable if connected to broader networks", "They cannot be connected to any network under any circumstances", "They have no relevance to cybersecurity at all"], answer: 1, exp: "Many SCADA systems predate modern cybersecurity practices, creating real risk as they get connected to broader networks." },
  { id: "sq-08", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "An 'air-gapped' SCADA network refers to:", options: ["A network with the fastest possible internet connection", "A network physically isolated from other networks (including the internet), intended to reduce exposure to external cyberattacks", "A network that is always publicly accessible", "A type of wireless mesh network"], answer: 1, exp: "Air-gapping is a physical isolation strategy to reduce remote attack surface, though it's not a complete guarantee of safety." },
  { id: "sq-09", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A successful cyberattack on a SCADA system controlling critical infrastructure (e.g., a power grid) is particularly dangerous because:", options: ["It only affects a single user's personal data", "It could disrupt real-world physical processes and essential services, not just digital data", "SCADA systems have no connection to physical processes", "It has no impact beyond a minor website outage"], answer: 1, exp: "Unlike typical IT breaches, a SCADA compromise can cause tangible, real-world physical/operational disruption." },
  { id: "sq-10", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "SCADA systems typically operate in:", options: ["Batch mode only, once per year", "Near real-time, continuously monitoring and controlling physical processes with timely data collection and command execution", "Completely offline with no data collection at all", "Only during scheduled maintenance windows"], answer: 1, exp: "Continuous, near-real-time operation is essential for effective industrial process monitoring and control." },
  { id: "sq-11", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "The ISO/IEC 25010 (successor to ISO/IEC 9126) software quality model defines quality in terms of characteristics such as:", options: ["Only the color scheme of the software", "Functional suitability, reliability, performance efficiency, usability, security, compatibility, maintainability, and portability", "Only the programming language used", "Only the marketing budget allocated"], answer: 1, exp: "ISO/IEC 25010 defines a comprehensive, multi-dimensional model of software product quality." },
  { id: "sq-12", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "'Functionality' (or functional suitability), as a software quality characteristic, refers to:", options: ["How visually appealing the software is", "The degree to which the software provides functions that meet stated and implied needs", "How fast the software runs", "How easily the software can be ported to another OS"], answer: 1, exp: "Functional suitability is about whether the software actually does what it's supposed to do." },
  { id: "sq-13", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "'Reliability', as a software quality characteristic, refers to:", options: ["How easy the software is to learn", "The software's ability to perform its required functions consistently under stated conditions for a specified period of time", "How much memory the software consumes", "How portable the software is across platforms"], answer: 1, exp: "Reliability concerns consistent, dependable operation over time under specified conditions." },
  { id: "sq-14", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "'Usability', as a software quality characteristic, refers to:", options: ["The software's raw processing speed", "How easily and effectively users can learn and operate the software to achieve their goals", "How much disk space the software occupies", "How many features the software has, regardless of ease of use"], answer: 1, exp: "Usability centers on the actual user experience of learning and operating the software." },
  { id: "sq-15", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "'Maintainability', as a software quality characteristic, refers to:", options: ["How fast the software processes data", "How easily the software can be modified to correct defects, improve performance, or adapt to a changed environment", "How visually attractive the interface is", "How many users the software currently has"], answer: 1, exp: "Maintainability is about the ease of making future changes to the software." },
  { id: "sq-16", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "'Portability', as a software quality characteristic, refers to:", options: ["How reliable the software is over time", "How easily the software can be transferred/adapted from one environment (hardware/OS/platform) to another", "How usable the interface is for a novice user", "How well the software integrates with a specific single database only"], answer: 1, exp: "Portability measures how well the software adapts across different hardware/OS/platform environments." },
  { id: "sq-17", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "'Efficiency' (performance efficiency), as a software quality characteristic, refers to:", options: ["How many features the software has", "The amount of resources (time, memory, etc.) the software uses to perform its functions under stated conditions", "How easy the software's source code is to read", "How the software is marketed to customers"], answer: 1, exp: "Performance efficiency concerns resource consumption relative to the work the software accomplishes." },
  { id: "sq-18", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "CMMI (Capability Maturity Model Integration) is a framework used to:", options: ["Only design user interfaces", "Assess and improve an organization's software/process development maturity across defined levels", "Only manage hardware procurement", "Only track individual employee salaries"], answer: 1, exp: "CMMI provides a structured way to evaluate and mature an organization's development processes." },
  { id: "sq-19", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "CMMI's maturity levels progress (from least to most mature) roughly as:", options: ["Optimizing, Defined, Managed, Initial, Quantitatively Managed", "Initial, Managed, Defined, Quantitatively Managed, Optimizing", "Managed, Initial, Optimizing, Defined, Quantitatively Managed", "There is only a single CMMI maturity level"], answer: 1, exp: "This is the standard CMMI staged representation, from ad hoc (Initial) to continuously improving (Optimizing)." },
  { id: "sq-20", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "At the 'Initial' CMMI maturity level, an organization's processes are typically:", options: ["Fully standardized and consistently applied across all projects", "Ad hoc, unpredictable, and poorly controlled, often dependent on individual heroics rather than defined processes", "Continuously optimized using quantitative feedback", "Managed with rigorous, organization-wide quantitative metrics"], answer: 1, exp: "'Initial' is the least mature level, characterized by unpredictable, reactive processes." },
  { id: "sq-21", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "At the 'Optimizing' CMMI maturity level, an organization:", options: ["Has no defined processes at all", "Continuously improves its processes based on quantitative feedback and innovative practices", "Only manages processes on a per-project, ad hoc basis", "Is at the very lowest level of process maturity"], answer: 1, exp: "'Optimizing' is CMMI's highest level, focused on continuous, data-driven process improvement." },
  { id: "sq-22", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "Software quality metrics such as 'defect density' measure:", options: ["The total revenue generated by the software", "The number of known defects relative to the size of the software (e.g., defects per thousand lines of code)", "The number of developers who worked on the project", "The number of meetings held during development"], answer: 1, exp: "Defect density normalizes defect counts by software size, enabling comparison across modules/releases." },
  { id: "sq-23", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "MTBF (Mean Time Between Failures), used as a software/system reliability metric, measures:", options: ["The average time it takes to fix a defect", "The average time a system operates correctly between one failure and the next", "The total number of defects ever found", "The average processing speed of the CPU"], answer: 1, exp: "A higher MTBF indicates a more reliable system with fewer failures over time." },
  { id: "sq-24", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "Code review, as a software quality practice, involves:", options: ["Only running the code once to see if it compiles", "Having other developers examine source code to identify defects, enforce standards, and share knowledge, before or alongside testing", "Only occurring after the software has already been deployed to production", "Replacing the need for any automated testing"], answer: 1, exp: "Code review is a human-driven quality practice, complementing (not replacing) automated testing." },
  { id: "sq-25", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "Static analysis tools improve software quality by:", options: ["Only running the compiled program at full speed", "Automatically examining source code (without executing it) to detect potential bugs, code smells, or security vulnerabilities", "Only checking the software's marketing materials", "Only measuring the software's disk usage"], answer: 1, exp: "Static analysis examines code without running it, catching certain classes of issues early and automatically." },
  { id: "sq-26", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "Technical debt, as a software quality concept, refers to:", options: ["The literal monetary loan a company takes to fund development", "The implied future cost of additional rework caused by choosing an easy/quick solution now instead of a better, more thorough approach", "A metric measuring how many developers are on the team", "The total number of servers a company operates"], answer: 1, exp: "Technical debt is a metaphor for the future cost of taking shortcuts in the present." },
  { id: "sq-27", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "Accumulating excessive technical debt over time tends to:", options: ["Speed up all future development indefinitely with no downside", "Slow down future development and increase maintenance costs, as the codebase becomes harder to work with", "Have no effect whatsoever on development speed", "Automatically resolve itself without any intervention"], answer: 1, exp: "Unmanaged technical debt compounds, making the codebase progressively harder and slower to change." },
  { id: "sq-28", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "ISO 9001 is a widely recognized standard primarily concerned with:", options: ["Software-specific security vulnerabilities only", "General quality management systems (applicable across industries), rather than being software-specific like ISO/IEC 25010", "Only mobile app UI design guidelines", "Only cellular network standards"], answer: 1, exp: "ISO 9001 is a broad, industry-agnostic quality management standard, distinct from software-specific quality models." },
  { id: "sq-29", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A key difference between SCADA and a typical general-purpose IT/business application is that SCADA systems:", options: ["Have no connection to any physical equipment whatsoever", "Directly interact with and control physical industrial processes/equipment in real time, where failures can have physical-world safety consequences", "Are always simpler and lower-stakes than typical business applications", "Never require any real-time data processing"], answer: 1, exp: "SCADA's direct control over physical processes raises the stakes of failures well beyond typical IT applications." },
  { id: "sq-30", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "'Security', as a software quality characteristic in ISO/IEC 25010, particularly matters for SCADA systems because:", options: ["Security is irrelevant to industrial control systems", "A security failure could allow unauthorized control over physical infrastructure, posing safety and operational risks beyond typical data breaches", "SCADA systems have no data worth protecting", "Security only matters for consumer mobile apps"], answer: 1, exp: "The physical consequences of a SCADA security breach make this quality characteristic especially critical for such systems." },
  { id: "sq-31", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A telemetry/communication network in a SCADA system is responsible for:", options: ["Only displaying marketing dashboards", "Carrying data and control signals between field devices (RTUs/PLCs) and the central monitoring/control system", "Only storing historical financial records", "Only compiling application source code"], answer: 1, exp: "The telemetry network is the communication backbone connecting field devices to the central SCADA system." },
  { id: "sq-32", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "Modern SCADA security best practices often recommend:", options: ["Removing all security measures for simplicity", "Network segmentation and monitoring (rather than relying solely on air-gapping), since many SCADA systems now require some level of connectivity for remote monitoring/business integration", "Connecting every SCADA system directly and unrestricted to the public internet", "Disabling all logging and monitoring capabilities"], answer: 1, exp: "As pure air-gapping becomes less practical, layered network segmentation and active monitoring are increasingly emphasized." },
  { id: "sq-33", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A key reason software quality models like ISO/IEC 25010 define multiple distinct quality characteristics is:", options: ["Because 'quality' is a single, simple, one-dimensional concept", "Because 'quality' is multi-dimensional; a system could be highly functional yet poorly usable, or reliable yet insecure, so each aspect needs separate consideration", "Because regulators require exactly eight characteristics with no justification", "Because a single metric would be more accurate than multiple characteristics"], answer: 1, exp: "Multiple independent characteristics are necessary because software can excel in one dimension while failing in another." },
  { id: "sq-34", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "An organization at CMMI's 'Managed' level, compared to 'Initial', typically has:", options: ["No project management processes of any kind", "Basic project management processes in place (planning, tracking) though still largely reactive rather than standardized across the organization", "Fully optimized, continuously improving processes organization-wide", "Processes that are identical in maturity to 'Initial'"], answer: 1, exp: "'Managed' represents a step up from ad hoc chaos, but processes are still typically project-specific rather than organization-wide." },
  { id: "sq-35", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "An organization at CMMI's 'Defined' level, compared to 'Managed', has:", options: ["Fewer defined processes than at the 'Managed' level", "Organization-wide standardized processes that are documented and consistently applied across projects, not just managed per-project", "No documented processes whatsoever", "The exact same maturity as the 'Initial' level"], answer: 1, exp: "'Defined' extends process discipline from individual project management to consistent, organization-wide standards." },
  { id: "sq-36", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "'Compatibility', as a software quality characteristic, refers to:", options: ["How fast the software executes", "The degree to which the software can exchange information with other systems and/or perform its functions while sharing a common environment/resources", "How visually appealing the software's interface is", "How many lines of code the software contains"], answer: 1, exp: "Compatibility concerns interoperability and peaceful coexistence with other systems/environments." },
  { id: "sq-37", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "Quality assurance activities applied to a SCADA project should particularly emphasize:", options: ["Skipping testing to speed up delivery", "Rigorous testing and validation, given the potential real-world safety and operational consequences of failures in industrial control systems", "Only cosmetic UI polish, since operators don't care about functionality", "Ignoring security entirely to reduce development cost"], answer: 1, exp: "Given the physical-world stakes, SCADA QA must be especially thorough compared to typical low-risk software." },
  { id: "sq-38", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A key motivation for adopting a formal software quality model (rather than relying on informal judgment) is:", options: ["Formal models make quality assessment less consistent", "Providing a consistent, measurable, and comparable way to define and assess quality across projects/organizations", "Formal models eliminate the need for any actual testing", "Informal judgment is always more reliable than any formal model"], answer: 1, exp: "Formal models bring consistency and comparability that purely informal, ad hoc judgment cannot provide." },
  { id: "sq-39", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "SCADA systems historically prioritized availability and real-time operational continuity over:", options: ["Basic functional correctness", "Strong cybersecurity hardening, since many were originally designed for isolated environments before broader network connectivity became common", "Any form of data collection", "Physical equipment integration"], answer: 1, exp: "This historical design priority (availability over security hardening) is a root cause of many modern SCADA security concerns." },
  { id: "sq-40", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A software 'defect density' metric is most useful for:", options: ["Determining the exact cause of any single specific bug", "Comparing relative quality/complexity across different modules or releases of software, when normalized by size", "Replacing the need for any code review", "Measuring the software's visual design quality"], answer: 1, exp: "Defect density is a comparative, size-normalized metric, not a tool for diagnosing individual bugs." },
  { id: "sq-41", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "The main purpose of measuring software quality (via metrics, models, standards) is to:", options: ["Increase bureaucracy with no practical value", "Provide objective, actionable insight into how well software meets its intended purpose and stakeholder expectations, guiding improvement efforts", "Replace the need for any actual software development", "Only satisfy regulatory paperwork requirements with no other benefit"], answer: 1, exp: "Quality measurement exists to inform real, actionable improvement, not just for its own sake." },
  { id: "sq-42", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A SCADA system's HMI dashboard commonly displays:", options: ["Only unrelated social media content", "Real-time status of monitored equipment/processes (e.g., pressure, flow rate, alarms) to operators for situational awareness and control", "Only historical financial reports from unrelated departments", "Only the source code of the control software"], answer: 1, exp: "The HMI's core purpose is giving operators real-time situational awareness of the monitored physical process." },
  { id: "sq-43", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "Refactoring code to pay down technical debt is generally easier and safer when:", options: ["No tests exist at all, so nothing constrains the changes", "A solid automated test suite exists, since tests can quickly verify that behavior hasn't been unintentionally changed", "The code is never actually run or executed", "The team has no understanding of the existing codebase"], answer: 1, exp: "A strong test suite provides a safety net, letting developers refactor with confidence that behavior is preserved." },
  { id: "sq-44", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A key trade-off organizations must manage regarding technical debt is:", options: ["There is no trade-off; debt should always be eliminated immediately regardless of cost", "Balancing the short-term speed benefit of taking shortcuts against the long-term cost of reduced maintainability if debt isn't periodically addressed", "Technical debt never has any real long-term consequences", "Technical debt only ever benefits a project with no downside"], answer: 1, exp: "This short-term speed vs. long-term maintainability trade-off is central to managing technical debt responsibly." },
  { id: "sq-45", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "CMMI assessments are often used by organizations (e.g., in government/defense software contracting) to:", options: ["Only decorate an office wall with no practical use", "Demonstrate a verified level of process maturity to clients/regulators as part of vendor qualification", "Replace the need for any actual software delivery", "Only apply to organizations with fewer than five employees"], answer: 1, exp: "CMMI ratings are often used as a credible, externally verifiable signal of process maturity in vendor selection." },
  { id: "sq-46", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "An 'alarm' in a SCADA system typically signals:", options: ["That everything is operating perfectly with no issues", "That a monitored parameter has crossed a defined threshold or an abnormal condition has been detected, requiring operator attention", "A scheduled software update notification only", "A marketing promotion for new equipment"], answer: 1, exp: "Alarms are a core SCADA feature, alerting operators to conditions needing prompt attention." },
  { id: "sq-47", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A key reason SCADA and other industrial control systems are increasingly targeted by cyberattacks is:", options: ["They are considered low-value targets with no strategic importance", "Their critical role in essential infrastructure, making a successful attack highly impactful/attractive to attackers (state actors, criminals) compared to typical IT systems", "They are impossible to attack under any circumstances", "Attackers have no interest in physical infrastructure"], answer: 1, exp: "The outsized real-world impact of a successful SCADA attack makes such systems particularly attractive targets." },
  { id: "sq-48", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "Comparing 'quality control' activities to the broader software quality model characteristics, QC activities like testing primarily aim to verify:", options: ["Only the software's marketing materials", "That specific instances of the software meet defined quality characteristics (e.g., that a build is sufficiently reliable/functional) before release", "Only the organization's overall process maturity level", "Nothing related to the actual product being built"], answer: 1, exp: "QC activities check whether an actual product instance meets the intended quality characteristics." },
  { id: "sq-49", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "A comprehensive software quality strategy for a SCADA-like critical system would typically combine:", options: ["Only cosmetic UI polish with no other considerations", "Rigorous testing/QA, strong security practices, and process maturity (e.g., via CMMI-aligned practices), given the elevated stakes of failure", "Only marketing efforts with no technical quality work", "Ignoring quality entirely to minimize development cost"], answer: 1, exp: "Given SCADA's real-world stakes, a robust quality strategy must combine multiple complementary practices, not rely on any single one." },
  { id: "sq-50", topic: "se-quality-scada", q: "Overall, software quality models (ISO/IEC 25010, CMMI) and domain-specific systems like SCADA both illustrate that:", options: ["Quality is a matter of pure luck with no way to systematically manage it", "Quality is a multi-faceted, measurable property of software/systems that must be deliberately engineered and continuously managed, not left to chance", "Quality only matters for consumer-facing mobile apps, never industrial systems", "There is only one single, universal way to measure quality with no nuance"], answer: 1, exp: "Together, these frameworks reinforce that quality is something to be deliberately engineered, measured, and managed throughout a system's life." }

);
